vels so you tell the  story of what's happening with black  women and certainly the racism has a  impact as well  but then you also get at the overall  health care system in general that where  everybody is impacted by we really had a  shared vision about and that was one of  the wonderful things about our  collaboration from the first time that  we sat down and started talking i think  we both understood how we wanted to tell  this story and we did not want it to be  a doom and gloom story we wanted to show  that there are solutions to the problem  dignified and safe birthing should be  first and foremost i think for for me  and for paula you know we're hoping that  there's a cultural shift in how we think  about birth right i think often the  first thing that comes to people's minds  first it's like oh i'm so excited for  you oh that's going to be really painful  you know so instead of having the  conversation about the pain of birthing  all the time or are the negative sides  that we really want people to come away  feeling hopeful excited inspired you  know moved by the film uh to action  through the the filming of  felicia's birth specifically and her  birth journey we wanted to reclaim what  birth looks like in film it usually  doesn't look like that it's a woman  screaming and ranting and she's in a  hospital and it's like this like  horrible thing and we really took our  time to show  a real process it's a long scene and and  we did that purposely so we wanted to  really show what it could be like she's  the home and what every woman deserves  you know we want  birthing people families women to to  to know that they have choices around  birthing options they can choose what  kind of health care provider they want  maybe they want a midwife you know maybe  they want an ob um there's different  birth settings that that they can choose  from so people don't realize that  there's other ways to do this every  other industrialized country has choice  and their rates are far better than ours  and we have these horrible rates because  they force everyone into a system as  neil dr shaw says in our film obese are  trained surgeons labor and delivery  floors are set up like icu's you know 99  of pregnant people you put them there  you're gonna see a lot of surgery  pregnancy is not a one size fits all so  we hope to show choice i really hope  there's accountability within the  medical system hospitals administrators  insurance companies providers you know  they need to provide human-centered care  i was pregnant myself through making  this film the first part of it and then  postpartum it was a pandemic my daughter  was born in march of 2020. that time  period was was especially hard i did  speak to someone you know a doula  therapist once a week to decompress and  really try to you know i try to separate  the work from from the personal life but  as tanya said it was a heavy time in the  world um we're covering heavy subject  matter and i really just took strength  from the team  making the film with us from the  protagonist from omari shawnee and bruce  and when i you know shifted that  mindset of like despair you know and  shifted it towards you know this film  like action and and the impact the film  can make then it like you know charged  me up to uh to focus on that part of  this you know i hope people come away  understanding that the u.s maternal  mortality crisis uh how it's affecting  black women is all part of the overall  reproductive justice conversation you  know there really is an attack on women  and um it's not just abortion rights  it's also birthing rights and birthing  choices and options so i hope people  understand that this is all part of one  conversation and we have to care about  our women and that's how we care about  our families and that's how we care  about our communities  [Music]
 

